Sturge-Weber syndrome
Disabilities & Congenital Disorders
Facial port-wine stain in V1 distribution, Refractory seizures, Contralateral hemiparesis, Ipsilateral glaucoma

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DCD-048
Reason for Visit: Acute refractory focal seizure cluster and prolonged post-ictal lethargy.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a 5-year-old child with Sturge-Weber syndrome experiencing a cluster of three focal motor seizures affecting the right side of the body over the past 90 minutes, with the child remaining unresponsive and profoundly lethargic between episodes. Assessment could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period due to the critical risk of non-convulsive status epilepticus, acute ischemic stroke (stroke-like episode), and the need for emergent pharmacological intervention to break the seizure cycle.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: They keep having seizures on their right side, and they won't wake up at all between the fits.
History of Present Illness: The parents report a 90-minute history of acute, severe neurological decompensation. The patient has a known diagnosis of Sturge-Weber syndrome, characterized by a congenital facial port-wine stain (nevus flammeus) strictly involving the ophthalmic (V1) distribution of the left trigeminal nerve. The patient has a history of early-onset, refractory seizures and baseline right-sided (contralateral) hemiparesis. Today, the patient experienced three distinct focal clonic seizures of the right arm and leg, each lasting 3-4 minutes. The parents administered the home rescue medication after the second seizure, but a third seizure occurred 20 minutes ago. The patient currently exhibits severe behavioral unresponsiveness and profound lethargy.
Functional Status: Ambulant with a right-sided limp; currently in a deep post-ictal or non-convulsive status state.
Social/Safety Concerns: Immediate risk of permanent hypoxic brain injury and stroke-like deterioration.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/65 mmHg | HR: 135 bpm | Temp: 37.5 C | RR: 26 bpm | SpO2: 96% | Wt: 20.0 Kg
General: Child is poorly responsive to physical stimuli, appearing deeply lethargic with shallow respirations.
Physical Exam: Facial examination reveals the classic left-sided V1 port-wine stain extending over the forehead and upper eyelid. Ocular exam reveals left eye buphthalmos consistent with known ipsilateral glaucoma. Neurological examination demonstrates profound flaccidity and dense hemiplegia of the right upper and lower extremities (worse than baseline hemiparesis). During examination, the patient begins a fourth focal clonic seizure involving rhythmic twitching of the right face and right arm. Pupils are equal but sluggish. Cardiovascular and respiratory exams are unremarkable.
Environmental Observations: Rescue medication packaging (buccal midazolam) is empty on the counter.
Medication Reconciliation: Routine daily anticonvulsants administered; one dose of PRN buccal midazolam was given 45 minutes ago with failed efficacy.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Sturge-Weber syndrome (complicated by refractory focal status epilepticus) - Status: Acute, critical complication
Differentials:

Acute Ischemic Stroke - A known, frequent complication in this syndrome due to the underlying vascular malformation, which can present identically to or concurrently with the prolonged seizure cluster and Todd's paresis. Requires emergent MRI.

Meningitis/Encephalitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of high fever or nuchal rigidity, and the stereotypic, focal nature of the seizures directly matching the patient's known structural brain disease.

Complex Migraine - Ruled out/unlikely due to the objective presence of continuous focal clonic motor activity and profound, unrelenting altered consciousness.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Extreme (Neurological emergency)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented in refractory focal status epilepticus. The objective finding of a fourth focal seizure occurring despite the administration of a first-line home rescue benzodiazepine confirmed that the seizure cycle was medically refractory. This assessment could not be safely delayed because continuous seizure activity in the structurally abnormal, under-perfused cerebral cortex of a Sturge-Weber patient rapidly leads to irreversible ischemic damage (stroke-like episodes) and permanent worsening of their hemiparesis. Immediate emergency transfer to a pediatric intensive care unit for intravenous second-line anticonvulsant loading and neuroimaging was mandatory.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Positioned the patient in the lateral recovery posture to secure the airway during the ongoing focal seizure. Administered a stat dose of intramuscular Levetiracetam as a second-line agent to attempt to break the refractory cycle while awaiting transport. Activated 000 for immediate emergency ambulance transport to the Paediatric Emergency Department.
Positive Behavioral Change: N/A - Acute life-threatening emergency requiring immediate transfer.
Home-Based Services (Optional): N/A.
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A.
Follow-up: Handover provided directly to the responding paramedic crew and receiving pediatric neurology team.

