PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Seizure control in Sturge-Weber syndrome is notoriously difficult; prolonged clusters often result in a temporary or permanent worsening of their baseline weakness.
Clinical Course: The patient will require admission for IV anticonvulsants, continuous EEG monitoring, and likely an urgent MRI to rule out acute ischemia in the brain tissue underlying the vascular malformation.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to give the child any food, water, or oral medications. Keep them firmly in the recovery position to prevent choking on saliva.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The abnormal blood vessels on the surface of their brain are causing an electrical storm that will not stop. Because the home rescue medicine failed to break the cycle, they are in a dangerous state called status epilepticus. The injection I gave provides a different type of chemical brake, but they must go to the hospital immediately for intravenous medicines to stop the seizures and protect their brain from permanent damage.
Actionable Steps: Gather all their neurology records. Do not feed them. Follow the paramedics' instructions carefully.

